{
  "version": 1,
  "title": "What If the Internet Disappeared for 30 Days? Cash Isn't Enough",
  "brief": "A self-contained fictional five-minute scenario following delivery worker Ada and shopkeeper Milo through thirty days without routable public Internet. Electricity and local networks remain; radio can work, while telephone availability varies by system. Clearly label the premise as hypothetical, not a forecast or a realistic single kill switch. Follow failed orders, payment friction, supply coordination, paper routes, verified notice boards and community radio, then a gradual reconnection. Cool layered city, physical network links and packets, an offline checkout, animated supply chain and radio desk. Suspenseful but humane; the surprise is that coordination matters as much as cash.",
  "niche": "What-if & Survival",
  "requested_minutes": 5.0,
  "seed": 903,
  "provenance": "reviewed-plan",
  "factual_status": "fiction",
  "sources": [
    {
      "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1958",
      "claim": "Architectural Principles of the Internet: interconnection of networks and end-to-end design. This supports the narrow premise explanation; no RFC predicts the invented thirty-day timeline or its social consequences."
    }
